/// A function recurses polymorphically over a nested data type, and its writer
/// states its signature.
///
/// A nested type's tail holds pairs of its elements, so each recursive call
/// runs at a larger type. The checker decides such a call against a stated type,
/// and no algorithm infers that type.
///
/// The writer states the signature. The recursive call then checks at the
/// pair type.
///
/// - see: #4565 — the loops sketch, its rulings in the issue body

/// A list whose every next cell holds pairs of the cell before.
data Nest(A : Type) : Type where {
  /// The empty nest.
  | Nil : Nest(A)
  /// An element, then a nest of pairs.
  | Cons : (head : A, tail : Nest(A * A)) --> Nest(A)
}

/// The number of elements a nest holds. The recursive call runs at `A * A`,
/// and the stated signature lets it check.
def size[A : Type](xs : Nest(A)) -> Nat {
  match xs {
    | Nil => 0
    | Cons(_, tail) => 1 + 2 * size(tail)
  }
}
